tally up all the Grand Slam  championships won by current WTA players  and you'll notice that nearly half  belonged to just one person Serena  Williams in fact Serena is one of the  most successful tennis players of all  time and that's in large part thanks to  how iconic serve you can break Serena's  serve into four parts the acceptance  window is the area of the car where the  ball has to land in order for the serve  to count it's determined by the minimum  angle for the ball to clear the net and  the maximum angle for it to land in  bounds so you can see here that the  height at which you hit the ball makes a  big difference as polar you are you have  more acceptance window imagine Yahoo  Ming learns play tennis and if he hits  the serve he will have a huge acceptance  window Serena isn't nearly as tall as  yeah and she's not even among the  tallest female tennis players out there  but she makes up for it so Serena  Williams does exceptionally well at the  position when she hits her arm is fully  stretched and then she has a little bit  of jump the stretch and jump boost the  height at which she hits the ball  increasing her acceptance window and  ultimately improving her chances of  hitting her in bounds now Serena isn't  the only player who extends on the serve  but according to experts she is much  more consistent and it pays off over the  last 10 years she's won 82 percent of  the games that she served that's a  whopping 11 percent higher than the  average of the top 20 female players  over the same time frame but of course  serving the ball in bounds is just the  first step what gets her all those wins  is the fact that she makes her service  extremely difficult to return that's  where power and topspin coming  Serena's power serve is one of the  fastest in the game averaging 170 km/h  that's about 12 kilometers per hour  faster than the average female  professional tennis player now part of  that speed is the result of pure  strength Serena Williams you can just  see she has like incredible ly muscular  body so she can generate a huge amount  of force but what really sets arenas  power serve apart is her timing to  maximize power on the serve tennis  players will bend their knees rotate and  drop the hip lower the shoulder and most  importantly twist at the trunk in other  words they use their whole body to gain  as much velocity behind the serve as  possible but none of that matters if you  don't hit the ball at the exact moment  when your arm is at full reach and your  body's in mid-swing in tennis this is  called fluidity and Serena is often  credited with the most fluid serve in  the spa  there will be no jerking motions you  will similarly hit the ball with maximum  power but there's a catch to these power  serves yes they're fast but the velocity  narrows the acceptance window making  them less reliable  that's why Serena also uses topspin to  her advantage  usually when her first power serve goes  out of bounds  now topspin serves a slower for example  Serena's average topspin serve is about  a hundred and forty-five km/h 25 km/h  slower than her power serve but topspin  serves are more accurate and still  difficult to return that's because the  topspin on the ball forces it to drop  sharply right before it reaches the  opponent that change in movement is  thanks to a phenomenon called the Magnus  effect as the ball spins through the air  it creates a pocket of lower pressure  underneath it and it's that low pressure  that makes the ball drop abnormally fast  catching many opponents off guard but  Serena's second serve is not the fastest  in the game Maria Sharapova for example  will fire her second serve at about a  hundred and fifty kilometers per hour  but Sharapova sacrifices accuracy for  speed here for comparison  Serena is scored on average two and a  half fewer double faults per match than  Sharapova over the last ten years so she  combines the first serve of high speed  which has a little bit more risky but  the her second serve is safer however it  has a high topspin it's awkward to  return and combining these two she  becomes excellent excellent service  player meaning she scores more aces on  average an ace is a successful serve  that the opponent racquet cannot reach  and therefore doesn't return over the  last 10 years  Serena's recorded four more aces per  match and the average top 20 WTF players  last but not least is the coup de Grasse  of Serena's serve disguise another  exceptional quality is her ability to  serve the ball without telling the  opponent what kind of service you will  make check out this ball toss normally  the way a player tosses the ball will  give their opponent a clue as to what  service coming but not Serena she tosses  the ball to the same spot with the same  body position every single time  her toss is so consistent and smooth  that she manages something most others  find impossible to replicate  she can disguise the serve so Sarina  could be about to deliver a topspin  serve a power serve or even a slicer yet  the opponent has no way of knowing  what's coming these things are all  disguised until the very last moment and  that makes Serena Williams serves  exceptionally difficult to return so far  as long as Serena's competing these  three words have the power to strike  fear into many pro-players hearts  Williams to serve
 

